920:<p id="Thmproofx10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We proceed with the description of the OR-cross-composition. For the sake of brevity, in this proof, we use <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-ONCF-coloring and ONCF-coloring interchangeably. We define a polynomial equivalence relation <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition6" title="Definition 6 (Polynomial equivalence relation []) ‣ 2.2 Parameterized complexity ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Parameterized Complexity of Conflict-free Graph Coloring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) as follows. Let 2 instances of <span id="Thmproofx10.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Clique</span> be equivalent under <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> if the graphs have the same number of vertices and they ask for a clique of the same size. It is easy to verify that <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial equivalence relation. Suppose we are given <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> instances of clique that are equivalent under <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, label them as <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},\ldots,X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},\ldots,X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let every instance have <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and ask for a clique of size <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, enumerate the vertices in each instance arbitrarily. We create an instance <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-ONCF-coloring by the following steps (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof ‣ 4.1 Kernel lower bounds for 𝑞-ONCF-Coloring ‣ 4 Kernelization ‣ Parameterized Complexity of Conflict-free Graph Coloring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for a sketch of <math id="Thmproofx10.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
